<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goitacá</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Goytacaz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Goitacá were an indigenous people of the coastal plain of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, around the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a> do Sul river between Espírito Santo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. Feared as warriors, they repeatedly attacked the early Portuguese settlements of the 16th century. They disappeared as a distinct people in the colonial period, but the city of Campos dos Goytacazes preserves their name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goitaca.jpg" alt="Goitacá" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presença indígena na costa, século XVI, Brasil.jpg: Epalitot derivative work: Walrasiad (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_indigenous_peoples_of_Brazil_(16th_C.).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
